## How the closest papers are written

- *Elo Uncovered* (pairwise Elo for LLM evaluation, empirical, synthetic plus Arena-type data): intro of 715 words with ~25 distinct citations in its first two paragraphs; states the gap ("no comprehensive examination of the compatibility of Elo scores and LLMs"), the approach (two axioms, reliability and transitivity), the findings in words (sensitive to order and hyper-parameters, more so for close models) and an "Implications" paragraph. Five figures and two tables, first figure on page 2. Related work as its own section, "Empirical Guidelines" section, "Conclusion and Limitations".
- *Prediction-Powered Ranking of LLMs* (statistical framework on Arena votes): 1,289-word intro, four figures all in the experiments, no table, the Discussion and Limitations section is separate.
- *Most Influential Subset Selection* (data removal and influence, theory plus experiments): 579-word intro with a four-bullet contributions list and a "Concurrent work" paragraph; four figures; 76 distinct works cited in the main text.
- *Preference Learning Algorithms Do Not Learn Preference Rankings* (a surprising-measurement paper): abstract leads with a "Surprisingly, … less than 60 %" threshold; a named Limitations section.

All quoted phrases are from the proceedings PDFs of those papers.
